@startuml transition_cancellation_preemption

title Transition Cancellation / Preemption

box "Caller thread" #FAFAF0
participant "State Manager" as Client
end box

queue "Component Event Queue" as EventQueue

box "Main loop thread" #F0F4FA
participant "ProcessGroupManager" as PGM
participant "Graph" as Graph
participant "Transition" as Transition
end box

queue "Worker Queue" as WorkerQueue

box "Worker thread" #FAF6F0
participant "IComponentController" as Monitor
participant "IComponent" as Component
end box

participant "Operating System" as OS

note over Graph
  graph is in state kInTransition: some components are in flight
  on the workers, others are still waiting in the worker queue
end note

' --- preemption --------------------------------------------------------------
Client -> EventQueue: request another Run Target
EventQueue -> PGM ++: run target request
note over PGM
  A supervision failure and the shutdown of the Launch Manager
  preempt a running transition in exactly the same way.
end note

PGM -> Graph ++: set new pending Run Target
PGM -> Graph: cancel the running transition
note over Graph
  The graph moves to kInTransition -> kAborting and requests the
  stop token that was created when the transition started. Every
  component dispatched by this transition carries that token.
end note
PGM --> Client: request accepted
deactivate Graph
deactivate PGM

note over Graph
  From here no further component is dispatched: new components
  are only taken from the transition while the graph is in
  kInTransition.
end note

' --- effect on the components ------------------------------------------------
WorkerQueue -> Monitor ++: a task of the cancelled transition is executed by a worker
note over Monitor
  Where the cancellation finds the task decides what happens to
  the component.
end note

alt #F2FAF2 the task had not been started when the cancellation arrived
    Monitor -> Monitor: the stop token is already stopped
    Monitor -> EventQueue: job skipped
    note right of Monitor
      <b>Cancellation point 1</b>
      The component is not touched at all and keeps the state it
      had before the transition.
    end note

else #FFF8E8 Component Activation is between two startup attempts
    Monitor -> Component ++: activate
    Component -> Component: no further attempt is started
    Component -> Component: deactivate the component
    Component --> Monitor: activation failed
    Monitor -> EventQueue: component failed to activate
    note right of Monitor
      <b>Cancellation point 2</b>
      The process of the failed attempt is already gone, so only
      the component itself has to be deactivated. It is left
      stopped.
    end note
    deactivate Component

else #Pink Component Activation is waiting for its ready condition
    Monitor -> Component ++: activate
    Component -> Component: the wait runs until the ready condition is\nsatisfied or the ready timeout expires
    note right of Component
      <b>Not abortable</b>
      The same for every ready condition: the Running report, a
      configured file, or the termination of the process itself.
    end note
    opt the ready timeout expires
        Component -> OS ++: terminate the process that was started\n(SIGTERM, then SIGKILL after the shutdown timeout)
        OS --> Component --: process terminated
    end
    Component --> Monitor: the ordinary result of the attempt
    Monitor -> EventQueue: component activated or failed to activate
    note right of Monitor
      A component that reaches its ready condition is reported as
      activated and left running. An attempt that times out ends
      at cancellation point 2.
    end note
    deactivate Component

else #Pink the component is already waiting for a process to terminate
    Monitor -> Component ++: activate or deactivate
    Component -> Component: the wait for the process to exit is not interrupted
    opt the shutdown timeout expires
        Component -> OS ++: SIGKILL
        OS --> Component --: process terminated
    end
    Component --> Monitor: the ordinary result of the step
    Monitor -> EventQueue: component deactivated or failed to activate
    note right of Monitor
      <b>Not abortable</b>
      SIGTERM has already been sent and the process may be
      writing its state to disk, so it is always given its full
      shutdown timeout before SIGKILL.
    end note
    deactivate Component
end
deactivate Monitor

' --- settling ----------------------------------------------------------------
loop until every component in flight has reported back
    EventQueue -> PGM ++: component event
    PGM -> Graph ++: apply the component event
    note over Graph
      While the graph is kAborting a result is only counted. The
      transition is not advanced and nothing new is dispatched.
    end note
    deactivate Graph
    deactivate PGM
end

note over Graph
  Every component in flight reports back. The longest a
  cancellation can take is one ready timeout plus the
  termination of the process that follows it.
end note

note over Graph
  With the last component in flight the graph moves to
  kUndefinedState. Components that were already running are
  deliberately left running.
end note

' --- the next transition -----------------------------------------------------
PGM -> PGM ++: handle the graph state
PGM -> Graph ++: start the transition to the pending Run Target
Graph -> Transition ++: set up a transition to the Run Target
note over Transition
  The stop set of the new transition is derived from the live
  state of the components, so everything the cancelled transition
  left running is picked up and stopped if the new target does
  not need it.
end note
Transition --> Graph --: components ready to be stopped
deactivate Graph
deactivate PGM

legend bottom
  A cancellation is observed at two points, each of which leaves the component
  in a state the next transition can reason about:

  1. Before the task is executed. A worker that picks up a task whose stop token
     is already stopped does not run it and reports it as skipped. The component
     keeps the state it had before the transition.
  2. Between two startup attempts. The process of the failed attempt is already
     gone; no further attempt is started, the component is deactivated and the
     activation returns. The component is left stopped.

  Nothing else is interrupted:

  - The wait for a ready condition - the Running report, a configured file, or
    the termination of the process itself - runs until the condition is
    satisfied or the ready timeout expires. On the timeout the process that was
    started is terminated with SIGTERM and, after the shutdown timeout, SIGKILL.
  - A termination that has already been requested runs to its end. The process
    may be writing its state to disk, so it always keeps its full shutdown
    timeout before SIGKILL follows.

  A component that reaches its ready condition despite the cancellation is
  reported as activated and left running; the next transition stops it if its
  target does not need it. The time a cancellation needs is therefore bounded by
  the ready and shutdown timeouts of the components still in flight.
end legend

@enduml
